		<HdrMax>16</HdrMax>
	</TonemappingConfig>
	<MotionBlur> 
		<Enabled>true</Enabled>
		<!-- keep in mind that different graphics settings might have motion blur disabled. This is on top of your individual graphics settings. -->
		<OneDividedByExposureTime>35</OneDividedByExposureTime>
		<!-- As you know, digital video cameras have a framerate. They also have a shutter speed. For example, you might have framerate of 30fps and a shutter speed of 1/60 seconds. That means the electronic shutter of the camera is open for 1/60 seconds, then closed for 1/60 seconds, and so on. The motion blur depends on the framerate. FWsim renders with a variable framerate. Nevertheless, if we have variable motion blur it will look jittery, so we need to have a fixed exposure time. "50" means 1/50 seconds. -->
		<ExposureCorrection>0.8</ExposureCorrection>
		<!-- due to motion blur, the brightness of the particle is smeared across more pixels of the camera than usual. Therefore, every individual pixel gets dimmer. For example, imagine if a particle moved at super high speeds - then we would see a very long streak of light but it would not be very bright. This parameter defines if we want to simulate this effect realistically, or only a little bit, or not at all. Set it to ZERO in order to have the brightness be always the same (unrealistic). Set it to one in order to simulate reality (which is realistic but might not look right). Set it to values in-between to fade between the two extremes. -->
	</MotionBlur>
	<MainStarsShape>
		<!-- When you change these parameters, it is important to take into account that bloom will make the particles somewhat bigger. So try it with and without bloom, to get better understanding of the parameters. -->
		<WhiteRadius>0.15</WhiteRadius>
		<!-- This is the "center" area of the particle. Center means, it has maximum output (for example, with a color of 255/0/0, this defines how much of the particle is completely red. This does not mean it is red on the screen, because we simulate the camera characteristics and it might fade into white. -->
		<FalloffFactor>0.2</FalloffFactor>
		<!-- this determines, after the center, how we fade from white into black. If it is 1, then we do it linearly. If you set it to >1, then we go to black very quickly. If you set it to <1, then it's more smooth -->
	</MainStarsShape>
